## Revised Plan: `minifyMessages()` in Session-Restoration with ux-boundary chunking

### Problem
Two issues with current `restoration.ts`:
1. `messagesToText()` uses `JSON.stringify(msg)` — token-wasteful, inconsistent with the rest of the codebase
2. Chunking at the Message[] level can split mid-turn (chunk ends after `ax`, next starts with orphaned `to`), making each chunk's text not self-contained

### Architecture change: text-level weaving + ux-boundary chunking

**BEFORE:** Message[]-level weaving → Message[]-level chunking → `messagesToText` per chunk
**AFTER:** Message[]-level weaving → `minifyMessages` on full woven buffer → text-level chunking at `ux|` boundaries → summarize each text chunk

The weaving still happens at the Message[] level (pushing child narrative Messages into the lead buffer at ready-event positions). Then the entire woven buffer is minified to text in one pass. The text is then split at `ux|` boundaries into self-contained chunks, each summarized via the LLM.

**Why ux boundaries:** `ux|` marks a new user turn — the start of a conversational unit. A chunk from `ux|...` to the next `ux|` (exclusive) contains the user's request, the assistant's response, any tool calls and results — a complete, self-contained unit. Splitting here means no chunk starts with an orphaned `to|` or `ti|` line.

**Why minify the full buffer first, then chunk the text:** The child narrative (`ux|[name summary]...`) is already a `ux|` line in the minified text. It sits right before its ready-note (`ux|[MAIL] [READY]...`). Both are `ux|`-prefixed, so the boundary splitter naturally keeps each as the start of a new chunk. The child summary and the ready-note land in the same chunk — preserving their semantic link.

### Overflow safety
- `estimateTextTokens` (to be exported from `token.ts`) counts tokens from the **already-minified, already-truncated text** — the chunker sees exactly what the LLM will receive
- `truncateToolOutput: true` caps each `to|` line at `maxContentLength` chars — no pathological 10KB tool output can blow up a chunk
- Chunker fires at 50000 tokens of **actual minified text** → the LLM never receives more than ~50000 tokens per call
- Strictly safer than old `JSON.stringify` (which could exceed the estimate due to key names/escaping overhead)

